      <h5 id="learn-more">Where can I learn more about Causality and Causal Inference?</h5>
      <p>
        You can find out about many of the core concepts and ideas in our <a target="_blank"
          href="/articles/">Help articles</a>. They
        also explain some maths
        terminology we can’t avoid using, and have lots of links back to relevant content elsewhere on the Internet.
        We try to make Causal Wizard easy to use, but we don’t want to hide important words you might need later, if you
        want to discuss your results with a statistician or data scientist.
      </p>
      <p>
        You don’t <i>need</i> to read anything to start using Causal Wizard, but if you want to learn more about how it
        works
        and how to interpret your results, we recommend the following websites, videos and articles:
      </p>

      <h5 id="videos">Videos</h5>
      <p>
        For those wanting to be convinced about the importance of explicitly grappling with Causality:
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KNPYUVmY3NM"
              target="_blank">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KNPYUVmY3NM</a> <b>Science Before Statistics: Causal
              Inference</b>, by Richard McElreath.
          </li>
          <li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ts0hnNBRIWg"
              target="_blank">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ts0hnNBRIWg</a> <b>Introduction To Causal Inference And
              Directed Acyclic Graphs
            </b>, by Peter WG Tennant in a workshop for the UK Reproducibility Network (UKRN).
          </li>
          <li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DXBPtpBhGqo"
              target="_blank">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DXBPtpBhGqo</a> <b> A Brief Introduction to Causal
              Inference (Course Preview)</b>, by Brady Neal.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>


      <h5 id="books">Books</h5>
      <p>
        If you're not sure which book to read, check out this handy <a
          href="https://www.bradyneal.com/which-causal-inference-book" target="_blank">flowchart</a> which helps you
        decide!
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://theeffectbook.net/" target="_blank">https://theeffectbook.net/</a> <b>The Effect: An
              Introduction to Research Design and Causality</b>, by Nick Huntington-Klein.
          </li>
          <li><a href="https://matheusfacure.github.io/python-causality-handbook"
              target="_blank">https://matheusfacure.github.io/python-causality-handbook</a> <b>Causal
              Inference for The Brave and True</b>, by Matheus Facure Alves
            Alves.
          </li>



          <li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Book_of_Why"
              target="_blank">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Book_of_Why</a> <b>The book of Why</b> - history of the
            Do-Calculus and Pearlian approach to Causality. Authors Judea Pearl and Dana Mackenzie. <a
              href="https://books.google.com.au/books/about/The_Book_of_Why.html?id=EmY8DwAAQBAJ" target="_blank">E-book
              can be purchased online</a>.
          </li>
          <li><a href="https://xcelab.net/rm/statistical-rethinking/"
              target="_blank">https://xcelab.net/rm/statistical-rethinking/</a> <b>Statistical Rethinking
            </b>, by Richard McElreath. Note this is a course (wait list form available <a
              href="https://github.com/rmcelreath/stat_rethinking_2024?tab=readme-ov-file#format"
              target="_blank">here</a>) <a
              href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLDcUM9US4XdPz-KxHM4XHt7uUVGWWVSus" target="_blank">online
              lectures</a>, a <a href="https://github.com/rmcelreath/stat_rethinking_2023" target="_blank">Git
              repository</a> AND a book!
          </li>

          <li><a href="https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/miguel-hernan/causal-inference-book/"
              target="_blank">https://www.hsph.harvard.edu/miguel-hernan/causal-inference-book/</a> <b>Causal
              Inference: What If (the book)</b>, by Miguel Hernan.</li>
          <li><a href="https://alexdeng.github.io/causal/index.html"
              target="_blank">https://alexdeng.github.io/causal/index.html</a> <b>Causal Inference and its applications
              in Online Industry</b>. Written by Alex Deng</li>
          <li><a href="https://mixtape.scunning.com/" target="_blank">https://mixtape.scunning.com/</a> <b>Causal
              Inference
              The Mixtape</b>. Deep dive
            slides and articles about various topics in Causal Inference, by Scott Cunningham.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.amazon.com/Causal-Inference-Discovery-Python-learning/dp/1804612987"
              target="_blank">https://www.amazon.com/Causal-Inference-Discovery-Python-learning/dp/1804612987</a>
            <b>Causal
              Inference and Discovery in Python</b>: Unlock the secrets of modern causal machine learning with DoWhy,
            EconML,
            PyTorch and more. Written by Aleksander Molak.</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

