              Courses</b> -
            for scientists, prerequisites apply.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <h5 id="papers">Academic papers</h5>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7081045/"
              target="_blank">https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7081045/</a> Methods for Evaluating Causality
            in Observational Studies (2020)
          </li>
          <li><a href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w26104/w26104.pdf"
              target="_blank">https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w26104/w26104.pdf</a> Paper comparing
            the Potential Outcome and Causal Diagram approaches to Causality (2019).</li>
          <li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6831" target="_blank">https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6831</a> "Pearl's
            Calculus of Intervention Is Complete." Key paper showing that the <a
              href="https://causalwizard.app/inference/article/do-calculus">do-calculus</a> is both sufficient and
            complete. (2012)</li>

          <li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.04216" target="_blank">https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.04216</a> "DoWhy: An
            End-to-End Library for Causal Inference". A paper
            about DoWhy, the most important backend library used in Causal Wizard (2020).</li>
          <li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.15475" target="_blank">https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.15475</a> Causal
            Machine Learning: A Survey and Open Problems (2022)
          </li>
          <li><a href="https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/2515245917745629"
              target="_blank">https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/2515245917745629</a> Thinking Clearly About
            Correlations and Causation: Graphical Causal Models for Observational Data (2018)
          </li>
          <li><a href="https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11098-009-9450-2"
              target="_blank">https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11098-009-9450-2</a> What are randomised
            controlled trials good for? (2010)
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

