      <h5 id="software">Software tools</h5>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="https://www.pywhy.org/" target="_blank">https://www.pywhy.org/</a> Collection of Open-Source
            software tools for Causal Machine Learning, including DoWhy (see below) and <a
              href="https://econml.azurewebsites.net" target="_blank">EconML</a>.</li>
          <li><a href="https://www.pywhy.org/dowhy/v0.9.1/" target="_blank">https://www.pywhy.org/dowhy/v0.9.1/</a>
            Documentation for <b>DoWhy</b>, part of PyWhy. DoWhy is used by Causal Wizard</li>
          <li><a href="http://www.dagitty.net/dags.html" target="_blank">http://www.dagitty.net/dags.html</a>
            <b>DAGitty</b>, a <a href="https://causalwizard.app/inference/article/dag">Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)</a>
            editor which has some of the same features as Causal Wizard.</li>
          <li><a href="https://twitter.com/AleksanderMolak/status/1636666314776346625"
              target="_blank">https://twitter.com/AleksanderMolak/status/1636666314776346625</a>
            Twitter thread about the Python <b>Causality ecosystem</b>.</li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/quantumblacklabs/causalnex"
              target="_blank">https://github.com/quantumblacklabs/causalnex</a> <b>CausalNex</b>, a Python library for
            modelling and inference. CausalNex learns a Bayesian Network model structure from either data, or your
            domain knowledge in the
            form of a <a href="https://causalwizard.app/inference/article/dag">Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)</a>, or
            both. Documentation here: <a href="https://causalnex.readthedocs.io/en/latest/" target="_blank">
              https://causalnex.readthedocs.io/en/latest/</a> .
        </ul>
      </p>

